coop doctor: check that the box's isolation works

Usage:
  coop doctor

  Runs checks in a temporary project and reports what passed, failed, or
  could not be checked. Your files and credentials are not the test data.

  Checks secret hiding, host access, privileges, offline networking,
  task access, credential isolation, settings permissions, and fork handoff.

  Uses this project's ordinary built image, or the shared image if available.
  If neither exists, uses Alpine and names the checks it cannot perform.
  Run coop build --egress open, then coop doctor to test the Coop image.
  Filtered images are separate and are verified when a filtered run starts.

  Requires Git and a running container runtime. Honors COOP_RUNTIME.
  Lists abandoned boxes but does not remove them.

==============================================================================

coop approve: review and approve this project's requested access

Usage: coop approve [--case-exposure]

  Shows what .agent/project.yaml asks for, compared with your last approval.
  Confirm the changes to allow new runs to use them.
  If nothing changed, there is nothing to approve.

  If a filtered box is stuck because the project's services have no rules, it
  lists the services and their ports and offers to add and approve the rules.

OPTIONS
  --case-exposure  additionally review file exposure with mount protection

  Use it only when a launch refuses hidden files that stay readable under
  another letter case. Approval covers those paths and their future contents
  for agents and services; new paths need approval again.
  Runs with eBPF protection keep those files hidden without this approval.

  Running boxes keep the rules they started with. A running editor session
  (coop acp) that has network rules moves to a new box with these rules
  between prompts.
  Run this command in your terminal.

