coop acp: connect your editor to an agent running in a Coop box

Usage:
  coop acp [<target|preset>] [<options>]

EDITOR SETUP
  Set the editor's agent command to coop and its arguments to:

  ["acp"]
  ["acp", "claude"]
  ["acp", "codex:gpt-6-astra/high@personal"]
  ["acp", "frontier"]

  Your editor must support ACP, the Agent Client Protocol.

  Without an agent or preset, Coop starts the first signed-in provider in
  Claude, Codex, Gemini, Grok order, using its default account. This does not
  pin the choice: use the editor's selectors after connecting.
  No providers signed in? Run coop login <agent>, then reconnect your editor.

OPTIONS
  --peer <target>        start a read-only peer agent (no @account); repeatable

  --egress <mode>        network access: filtered (default), open or offline
  --allow-domain <host>  allow TLS to this exact host on port 443; repeatable
  --egress-rules <path>  add the network rules in this file

SWITCHING AGENTS
  Use the editor's Provider and Account controls to change who runs the session.
  A preset chooses its agents automatically. Select None in Preset to choose
  a provider or account yourself.

  Switching providers carries the conversation into a new agent session.
  Account changes keep the conversation with the same provider.

  With filtered networking, only compatible providers and presets are offered.
  Claude, Codex, Gemini and Grok support it. Gemini needs a Google sign-in or
  a Gemini API key, not Vertex AI. Switching never changes this session's
  network rules.

PROJECT BOX
  With filtered networking the editor connects even when this project's box
  cannot start yet. Messages are held, the thread says why, and the session
  moves onto the box once it is ready. Approved build files rebuild by
  themselves. New or changed ones need coop build, or /build in the thread,
  which shows where Coop saved them and gives a code that builds exactly
  those files. Unapproved network access needs coop approve, and a broken
  .agent/project.yaml needs fixing. A box that exits as it starts, or does
  not answer within a minute, is tried again every 30 seconds; the agent log
  says why.

TROUBLESHOOTING
  Set COOP_ACP_TRACE=1 in your editor's agent environment to record a trace.
  Trace files are saved as ~/.local/state/coop/acp-trace-<pid>.log and may
  contain your prompts and file contents.

  COOP_ACP_WARM=0 disables background boxes kept ready for provider switching.
  COOP_ACP_CARRY_TOKENS sets the conversation size carried between providers.

  After replacing the Coop binary, send SIGHUP to the specific ACP process
  to reload it. SIGINT and SIGTERM stop it.

For more details see:
  coop help models
  coop help presets
  coop help net

==============================================================================

coop sessions: let other applications start and manage agents

Usage: coop sessions <command>

COMMANDS
  connect     connect this machine to a controller
  doctor      check whether the local service is ready
  check       see whether this build can use the session data as it is
  quiescence  inspect queued and running work before a restart
  compact     back up session data and reduce its disk usage

CONNECT THIS MACHINE
  coop sessions connect --controller https://controller.example \e
    --token-file /path/to/token

  The controller supplies each session's code, context, and execution settings.
  Leave this command running; it starts the local service automatically.

Command options: coop help sessions <command>

==============================================================================

coop sessions doctor: check the local session service

Usage:
  coop sessions doctor [<options>]

  The command succeeds when the service responds and is ready for sessions.

OPTIONS
  --socket <path>  check this Unix socket
  --json           print the result as JSON

EXAMPLES
  coop sessions doctor
  coop sessions doctor --socket /path/to/control.sock

==============================================================================

coop sessions check: see whether this build can use the session data as it is

Usage:
  coop sessions check [--state <path>]

OPTIONS
  --state <path>  check this session-data directory

EXAMPLES
  coop sessions check
  coop sessions check --state /var/lib/coop/sessions

BEFORE UPGRADING A WORKER
  Stop the session service, then run this command with the new coop build.
  It reads the session data and changes nothing.

EXIT STATUS
  0  this build uses the data as it is, or a started service holds none yet
  1  the check could not finish: the service is still running, there is no
     session data at the path, or some data can't be read
  2  invalid arguments
  3  this build would convert the data; older builds can't open it afterwards
  4  this build can't use the data: it is too old or too new

DEFAULT SESSION DATA
  ~/.local/state/coop/sessions

==============================================================================

coop sessions quiescence: inspect queued and running work before a restart

Usage:
  coop sessions quiescence [--state <path>] [--json]

  Before restarting a worker, drain new submissions at the controller, then
  run this to see whether queued or running work remains. It reads the
  running service's data, including its write-ahead log, and never migrates
  it. Zero counts still need a graceful service shutdown to finish cleanup.

OPTIONS
  --state <path>  read this session-data directory
  --json          print the snapshot as JSON

EXIT STATUS
  0  no queued or running work remains in the session store
  1  the store is unavailable, damaged or uses an unsupported schema
  2  invalid arguments
  3  work remains

DEFAULT SESSION DATA
  ~/.local/state/coop/sessions

Guide: https://coop.dryga.com/session-api.html#restart-and-recovery

==============================================================================

coop sessions compact: back up session data and reduce its disk usage

Usage:
  coop sessions compact --backup <path> [--state <path>]

OPTIONS
  --backup <path>  save a verified database backup to a new file
  --state <path>   use this session-data directory

EXAMPLES
  coop sessions compact --backup /path/to/session-backup.sqlite

BEFORE RUNNING
  Stop the session service.
  Choose a new backup file outside the session-data directory.
  Its parent directory must already exist.

  The backup contains private session data. Keep it protected.

DEFAULT SESSION DATA
  ~/.local/state/coop/sessions

==============================================================================

coop sessions connect: run a worker for a remote controller

Usage: coop sessions connect --controller <https-url> [<options>]

OPTIONS
  --controller <https-url>  controller to trust and connect to
  --token-file <path>       enrollment token (only needed on first connection)
  --state <path>            private worker data directory
  --ca-file <path>          CA bundle for a controller's private TLS certificate

DEFAULT STORAGE
  ~/.local/state/coop/sessions

RUNNING
  The controller supplies each session's code, context, and execution settings.
  No local policy file or worker configuration file is needed.
  Coop exchanges the enrollment token for worker credentials it can renew,
  then removes the token.

  Leave this command running. Connection failures are retried automatically.
  Press Ctrl-C to disconnect and stop the local service it started.
  On restart, use the same controller and state directory; omit --token-file.

